th lighting  and programming to make that connection  stronger and and also bridging over this  site that was full of underground  infrastructure as well and so the idea  of lifting that center stem really came  into a necessity on this particular site  as we explore the site connections we  start to move into working with the  different different heights of these  towers is just the top piece and looking  at that so this project is ongoing  there's some really interesting  discoveries in terms of the solar we  found that the temperature was actually  higher on the smaller plates and so  we're relating our glass performance to  that discovery so we will have it's some  different performing glass different  types of glass in the overall building  and I think one that one of the things  about the tall buildings back to the  diagram is not just about the high end  aspect of residential high-rise living  in the city there's also a very strong  need to consider residential buildings I  think you know just for the life of the  city one project we worked recently what  was this an exhibition and that was  shown at MoMA and it was all about the  foreclosure crisis  so we were one of four architects asked  to reconsider housing given the  foreclosure crisis and and in light of  that is there a way that we could  rethink how to do public housing and our  project really it sewed together many  different strains of the research that  we've been doing in the office and we  are I'll show you the project and and  just the I think the exciting thing is  here that we are really looking for  testing some of these in the marketplace  as well and so the the whole idea of the  exhibit was to for us to really look at  what was happening in these inner-ring  suburbs so again focusing on a place  that we know very well I think that  there there is a temptation oftentimes  to to look far afield but there is so  much going on and so many issues right  in our own backyard we felt we knew this