e  ideas can come from anywhere we do have  to get stuff done so we work hard I mean  just like all architects but we try to  just you know have more fun doing it  but we do have martinis at camp but yeah  yeah yeah the way that research informs  I think it's with the very different  scales that we work on there are it's  the biggest challenge is figuring out  what you want to research you know  what's going to be relevant to that  project and you definitely will go down  and have tangents and blind alleys and  and things like that but we were  building a body of research as well and  one of the things that we're working on  is is the structure for how to access  the research that we've already created  in the office so that new people coming  in will be able to access it too so one  one habit that we have is for every  project there's a bibliography and so  the the bibliography starts Sunday one  and it gets added to you know sometimes  by our clients tell us what books that  they are looking at but what research  they're looking at and then you know  what happens oftentimes is the project  goes on hold and then you know it's a  new team is is coming on in two years it  starts up again so we always have this  when any person coming onto the project  has access to those initial things that  created the concept what was influential  and so anybody can get up to speed on  that research and so you don't feel like  you're coming into a project and like  now I just have to detail this you know  door or something it's it you really  have a chance to understand the  underpinnings of the project so we're  trying to find about more ways to do  that besides just the bibliography but  it is such an important part of our  practice that it's it's it's a it's a  key thing  okay all right  thank you
 

